# Retest 1: auth edge logs for the T-15 (register) and T-20 (forgot-password) UI tests, 2026-11-16.
# Source: mcp__Supabase__query_logs (edge_logs) on eqozjuaaajstrewygqxr. Headers trimmed.
2026-11-16T10:49:20Z POST 429 /auth/v1/signup   x_sb_error_code=over_email_send_rate_limit   (NEW address t-e2e-new@)       -> UI: "Too many attempts. Please wait a minute and try again."
2026-11-16T10:50:36Z POST 200 /auth/v1/signup                                                (EXISTING address a@; obfuscated) -> UI: "Check your email"
2026-11-16T10:50:44Z POST 200 /auth/v1/recover                                               (UNKNOWN address nobody-t20@)    -> UI: "If an account exists for that email, we've sent a link..."
2026-11-16T10:51:45Z POST 429 /auth/v1/recover  x_sb_error_code=over_email_send_rate_limit   (EXISTING address a@)            -> UI: "Too many attempts. Please wait a minute and try again."

Finding (D-5): while the project's email-send quota is exhausted, the server answers 429 only for requests
that would send an email (new signup, recover for a registered address) and 200 for those that would not
(existing-address signup, unknown-address recover). The UI maps 429 to a distinct message, so the outcome
reveals whether an email is registered (AC-11 / BR-5). The quota is project-wide and small on the default
SMTP, so an attacker can exhaust it deliberately.

